<TopicPage slug="subjects/psychology/intermediate/perception">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Perception research asks <strong>how the brain builds the experienced world from sensory
    signals</strong> — and repeatedly finds construction where we assumed recording. Illusions
    are the field's instruments: cases where the inference machinery is exposed, running on
    inputs engineered to reveal its rules.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="inference, ambiguity, organisation, attention — the four pillars of perception" widget="equationStep" config={percepCfg} />

  <h2>The neuroscience handoff</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Senses → perception:</strong> the neuroscience ladder covers transduction and cortex; psychology studies the constructed result — what we experience and report.</li>
    <li><strong>Perceptual expertise is trainable:</strong> radiologists see things in scans novices cannot; chicken sexers, wine tasters, chess masters. Perceptual learning is real and specific.</li>
    <li><strong>Expectation shapes perception top-down:</strong> the same sound reads as "lah" or "far" depending on the sentence; priors (the Bayesian lesson) operate continuously in perception.</li>
    <li><strong>Cross-cultural findings temper universals:</strong> the Müller-Lyer illusion is weaker in non-carpentered environments — some perceptual "laws" are learned statistics of a built world.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Goldstein, Sensation and Perception; Simons & Chabris (1999); Henrich et al., BBS (2010).</p>
</TopicPage>
